window.__hvBdRegister("origen-first-principles", {"key":"origen-first-principles","sections":[{"i":0,"j":23,"loc":"Introduction; Preface 1–10","title":"The apostolic rule and open questions","summary":"An editorial introduction describes the treatise, its survival mainly in Rufinus's altered Latin, and Jerome's lost rival version. In the Preface Origen argues that, since believers in Christ disagree even on the gravest matters, a definite rule must be fixed from the teaching handed down from the apostles. He lists the points the apostles taught plainly: one creator God of both Testaments, Christ born of the Father and truly incarnate, the Holy Spirit equal in honour, the soul's judgment, free will, the devil, the world's beginning in time, and the hidden sense of Scripture. He also marks what they left open for lovers of wisdom: the Spirit's origin, the soul's origin, what came before and after this world, the nature of angels and stars, and whether God is incorporeal. He ends by urging the reader to build one connected body of doctrine from these elements.","ideas":["Church teaching handed down from the apostles is the test of truth","The apostles stated some doctrines plainly and left their grounds to be investigated","Free will is affirmed against astral determinism","Scripture has a spiritual meaning known only to those given grace"],"terms":[{"t":"asōmaton (incorporeal)","d":"A philosophers' term Origen notes is absent from Scripture, raising the question whether Scripture expresses the idea under another name."},{"t":"ecclesiastical and apostolical tradition","d":"The teaching kept in the churches in succession from the apostles, against which all opinions are measured."}]},{"i":24,"j":29,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 1.1–4","title":"God is not a body","summary":"Origen answers those who read Scripture as making God a body because God is called a consuming fire and a Spirit. He argues that God as light means the light that illuminates the understanding, and that God as fire consumes evil thoughts and desires, not wood or stubble. Scripture uses spirit for what is opposed to gross body, as in the contrast of letter and spirit and the veil over the heart. Participation in the Holy Spirit is compared to many sharing in the art of medicine, which shows that being shared does not imply being a body. Christ's words to the Samaritan woman call God Spirit to set Him apart from bodies, and truth to set Him apart from shadows.","ideas":["Light, fire and spirit in Scripture are read as intellectual, not bodily","Being shared by many does not require being a divisible body","Worship in spirit and truth replaces devotion to particular places"],"terms":[{"t":"letter and spirit","d":"Paul's contrast, read here as bodily versus intellectual or spiritual meaning."}]},{"i":30,"j":34,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 1.5–9","title":"God's incomprehensibility and the mind's kinship","summary":"Origen holds that God is incomprehensible and far greater than anything we perceive, as the sun exceeds a lamp's spark to weak eyes. We know Him from His works as from rays through a window. God is an uncompounded intellectual nature, a Monad, the source of all mind, needing no space, size or colour; the mind's own independence from place, shown by examples of seasickness and fever, supports this. He argues that mind is not a body, since the highest sense must have a real substance, and that the mind is God's intellectual image. Scripture's invisible God is never seen even by the Son but is known, and seeing God with a pure heart means understanding Him with the mind's divine sense.","ideas":["God surpasses all perception; we know Him from His works","God is simple and uncompounded, the source of all intellect","Seeing belongs to bodies; knowing belongs to intellectual beings","Bodily sense terms in Scripture name powers of the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"Monas / Henas","d":"Origen's Greek words for God as wholly one and without parts."},{"t":"divine sense","d":"The immortal, intellectual sense of the pure heart, by which the worthy may see God."}]},{"i":35,"j":42,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 2.1–6","title":"The Son as eternal Wisdom and Image","summary":"Origen separates Christ's deity as only-begotten Son from the human nature He assumed. The Son is Wisdom existing as a distinct hypostasis, not an impersonal quality, and God was never without Him, so the generation has no beginning in time or thought. Wisdom holds in advance the forms of all creation and, as Word, interprets its mysteries; He is also Life, Truth, Resurrection and the Way for those who fell. The generation is likened to brightness from the sun and to an act of will from the mind, without division of the divine nature, against those who imagine emanations. As image the Son is like Seth to Adam, the invisible image of the invisible God, through whom the Father is known.","ideas":["Wisdom is a living hypostasis, not an abstraction","The Son's generation is eternal, with no beginning","Generation as will from mind excludes physical division","The Son is the image by which we know the Father"],"terms":[{"t":"hypostasis (substantia)","d":"Distinct subsistent existence, ascribed to the Son as God's Wisdom."},{"t":"Only-begotten","d":"The Son as the one Son by nature, deriving from no other beginning than God."}]},{"i":43,"j":50,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 2.7–13","title":"Brightness, figure, and Wisdom's titles","summary":"Origen explains Hebrews' brightness of glory and express figure of person: the Son proceeds from God as brightness from light and gently trains weak eyes to bear the light. A comparison with a world-sized statue and a smaller exact copy shows how the Son, emptying Himself, reveals the Father's greatness. He then works through the Wisdom of Solomon's phrases: breath of God's power, which is power from power and never non-existent; efflux of the Almighty's glory, where God's omnipotence requires subjects and is exercised through the Son by reason, not force; splendour of eternal light; stainless mirror of God's working, since the Son does the same works; and image of His goodness. Primal goodness is in the Father, from whom Son and Spirit derive it, while creatures are good only by accident.","ideas":["The statue simile: a lesser copy reveals an unseeable greatness","God was always omnipotent, so always had subjects, and always Father","All things are subject through Wisdom by reason, not compulsion","Essential goodness belongs to the Trinity; creatures' goodness is accidental"],"terms":[{"t":"energeia (working)","d":"God's operative power in creation, providence and judgment, of which Wisdom is the stainless mirror."},{"t":"catachrestically","d":"By loose or borrowed usage, as when created things are called good."}]},{"i":51,"j":55,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 3.1–4","title":"The Holy Spirit known from Scripture","summary":"Origen notes that God the Father can be known in part from creation and some philosophers glimpsed a divine Word, but the Holy Spirit is known only from the law, prophets and Christian teaching. Scriptural testimonies show the Spirit's dignity: baptism is complete only in the name of Father, Son and Spirit, and blasphemy against the Spirit is unforgivable. Against claims of co-eternal matter or unbegotten souls, he holds that all things were created, yet finds no text calling the Spirit made. He reports his Hebrew teacher's reading of Isaiah's two seraphim as the Son and the Spirit and holds that the Spirit reveals God without having learned from the Son. Terms like always and was apply to the Trinity only by a stretch of language.","ideas":["Knowledge of the Spirit comes only through Scripture","The Spirit shares the Trinity's dignity in baptism","The Spirit never passed from ignorance to knowledge","Temporal words apply to the Trinity only loosely"],"terms":[{"t":"Trinity","d":"Father, Son and Holy Spirit, named together in saving baptism and held to be unchangeable."}]},{"i":56,"j":62,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 3.5–8; Chapter 4","title":"Distinct workings of Father, Son, Spirit","summary":"Origen asks why salvation needs the whole Trinity. The Father's working reaches all things, giving existence; the Son's reaches all rational beings, giving reason and with it liability to sin; the Spirit's is found only in saints turning to a better life. Sin against the Son of man may be forgiven as a lapse into folly, but one who has shared the Spirit and relapses blasphemes the Spirit; this does not make the Spirit greater, for nothing in the Trinity is greater or less. The goal is to grow so that one's nature becomes worthy of its Creator, with ever-increasing longing; if satiety comes, the fall is gradual and can be reversed. Chapter 4 illustrates falling away with a geometer or physician who forgets his art by neglect, then defers fuller treatment of rational beings.","ideas":["Existence from the Father, reason from the Word, holiness from the Spirit","Blasphemy against the Spirit is relapse after sharing the Spirit","Falling away is gradual, like forgetting a neglected skill","Blessedness should deepen longing, not bring satiety"],"terms":[{"t":"participation","d":"The sharing by which creatures receive existence, reason and holiness from the Persons of the Trinity."},{"t":"satiety","d":"Weariness of blessedness, which Origen names as the start of gradual decline."}]},{"i":63,"j":68,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 5","title":"Rational natures and the fallen powers","summary":"Origen lists the scriptural names of rational orders: angels, thrones, dominions, principalities, powers, the devil and his angels, spiritual wickedness, and the human nations allotted to angels. He asks whether these were created good or evil by nature or reached their state by merit, and argues that if evil powers were evil by creation, the blame would fall on the Creator. Ezekiel's second prophecy to the prince of Tyre, describing one stainless in paradise among the fiery stones until iniquity was found, he applies to a fallen angelic power that governed the Tyrians. Isaiah's Lucifer fallen from heaven and Christ's Satan falling like lightning show that the devil was once light. He concludes that only the Trinity is pure by essence, so happiness or ruin lies in our own conduct.","ideas":["Rank among rational beings comes from merit, not nature","The prince of Tyre is read as a fallen angelic power","Lucifer was once light, so evil was not his nature","No creature is pure or polluted by nature"],"terms":[{"t":"opposing powers","d":"Rational beings who fell from holiness and now resist those being trained in goodness."},{"t":"apostate","d":"A fugitive or deserter, Origen's gloss on the dragon in Job as the devil."}]},{"i":69,"j":74,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 6","title":"The end that resembles the beginning","summary":"Origen offers this topic as discussion rather than fixed doctrine. The end comes when all enemies are subjected to Christ, a subjection he reads as the saving obedience of the saints, and when Christ delivers the kingdom to the Father. Since the end is like the beginning, many varieties sprang from one beginning through differing lapses, and providence ranked beings as angels, powers, thrones and humans by merit. The fallen are not lost irrecoverably and may be remoulded, though the devil's party resists; he leaves to the reader whether they too will turn. On the visible world's passing he argues for a change of form, not destruction of matter, since only the Trinity exists without body.","ideas":["The end is like the beginning: unity, then diversity, then unity","Subjection to Christ means salvation, not mere defeat","Rational beings may pass through every order by free will","Bodily nature is transformed, not annihilated"],"terms":[{"t":"consummation","d":"The completion of things when God becomes all in all."}]},{"i":75,"j":84,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 7","title":"Are the sun and stars living beings?","summary":"Origen affirms as Church doctrine that all souls and rational natures are incorporeal yet created through Christ. He then asks whether sun, moon and stars are unchangeable, as some hold. Job's saying that the stars are not clean implies moral capacity, and God's commandment to the stars and their unerring order imply that they are living and rational. He suspects their souls were placed in their bodies from without, by analogy with Jacob and John the Baptist, whose souls acted before birth. Paul's creation subjected to vanity he reads as the stars clothed in bodies for their task of light, hoping for release, until all are handed to the Father.","ideas":["Heavenly bodies receive commands and move with order, so are rational","Their souls were implanted in their bodies from outside","Creation's subjection to vanity is the stars' service in bodies","The sun might echo Paul's wish to depart and be with Christ"],"terms":[{"t":"aplaneis (fixed stars)","d":"The non-wandering stars, distinguished from the planets by their kind of motion."},{"t":"vanity","d":"Paul's word, taken here to mean the body to which the stars are subjected."}]},{"i":85,"j":91,"loc":"Book I, Chapter 8","title":"Angels' offices earned by merit","summary":"Origen holds that angels such as Raphael, Gabriel and Michael, and the angels of churches and individuals, received their offices by merit shown before the world was formed, so God shows no partiality. He rejects those who posit different spiritual natures from different creators, pointing to Paul's persecution and Peter's denial as sins of so-called spiritual men. Every rational creature can admit good or evil, though not all do; only God, Christ and the Spirit cannot. He divides rational beings into the holy orders, the wicked powers who are unwilling rather than unable to return, and human souls who may be raised to angelic rank. He rejects the view that souls sink into beasts.","ideas":["Angelic offices reflect prior merit, preserving God's justice","Against rival creators of different natures","Capacity for evil does not mean every nature has done evil","Three orders of rational beings; transmigration into animals is rejected"],"terms":[{"t":"archangels","d":"Higher angels to whom distinct offices such as healing or war were assigned."}]},{"i":92,"j":102,"loc":"Book II, Chapters 1–2","title":"One world, created matter, and bodies","summary":"Origen traces the world's diversity to the varied lapses of rational beings from their first unity. God, without forcing anyone's will, fits these varied minds into one harmonious world, which is like one vast animal held together by God's power as by a soul. Bodily matter is without qualities in itself, receiving heat, cold, dryness and moisture, and is never found without them; he attacks the view that it is uncreated and co-eternal with God, citing Maccabees and the Shepherd for creation from nothing. Chapter 2 asks whether rational natures can ever be wholly bodiless; since only the Trinity can live without a body, matter is distinct from them only in thought and takes grosser or more celestial forms according to the beings it serves.","ideas":["Diversity in the world comes from diverse movements of free wills","The world is one great animal held together by God","Matter is created, not co-eternal with God","Only the Trinity lives without a body"],"terms":[{"t":"hylē (matter)","d":"The underlying substrate of bodies, without qualities of its own but never without them."}]},{"i":103,"j":109,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 3.1–5","title":"Worlds before and after this one","summary":"Origen asks whether worlds existed before this one and will follow it. To test whether matter could cease, he examines Paul's corruptible putting on incorruption, reading the soul clothed with Christ as the clothing of the body. He sets out the opposing view that at the end bodies grow finer until matter vanishes, then answers that free will could bring new movements and so require bodies again. He rejects worlds identical in every respect, comparing it to a measure of grain falling twice in the same pattern, and holds that worlds may be better, worse, or intermediate. From Paul he infers many ages before and after this one, which is the consummation of ages.","ideas":["The soul clothed with Christ is the body's incorruption","Free will rules out exactly repeated worlds","The grain-scattering image against identical recurrence","Many ages precede and follow this one"],"terms":[{"t":"age (aiōn)","d":"A world-period; the present one is called the consummation of many ages."}]},{"i":110,"j":111,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 3.6–7","title":"Meanings of world; three views of the end","summary":"Origen explains that kosmos means both world and ornament, and that Scripture uses it for the earth, for the universe, and for the other world Christ means by I am not of this world. He disowns a world of incorporeal ideas, preferring a better world perhaps within this one. He reports a view of spheres above the fixed sphere, a good land and higher heaven where the saints will live. He then sets out three views of the final state: wholly bodiless existence, bodies changed to an ethereal splendour, or ascent beyond the planetary spheres to the good land and heaven, and leaves the reader to judge.","ideas":["Kosmos means both world and ornament","Origen denies a world of Platonic ideas","The land of the living lies above the fixed sphere","Three possible accounts of final blessedness"],"terms":[{"t":"kosmos","d":"Greek for both world and ornament."},{"t":"aplanēs (non-wandering sphere)","d":"The sphere of the fixed stars, above which the abode of the saints is placed."}]},{"i":112,"j":116,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 4","title":"The God of the Law is Christ's Father","summary":"Origen refutes those who say Christ's Father is a different God from the God of the law and prophets. The Gospels' fulfilled prophecies, Christ's command to imitate the Father who sends sun and rain, His temple called His Father's house, and the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob all point to the Creator. The apostles' prayers and Paul's letters show the same God. To the claim that Moses saw God while Christ's God is unseen, he answers that a visible God would be a body; seeing God means understanding Him, so Moses saw only in part. Passages on God's anger, found also in Gospel parables, are read spiritually, since God is impassible.","ideas":["One God of Old and New Testaments","Christ's teaching cites the Creator as His Father","Seeing God means understanding Him","Divine anger is read spiritually; God is impassible"],"terms":[{"t":"impassible","d":"Wholly free from passions or affections such as anger or repentance."}]},{"i":117,"j":122,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 5","title":"Justice and goodness belong to one God","summary":"Origen answers the heretics who split justice from goodness, calling the Father of Christ good but not just and the God of the law just but not good. He argues that their own definitions fail: the Old Testament punishments do not fit their idea of strict justice, and the Gospels show the Father preparing fire and the king casting out the guest without a wedding garment. Arguing from reason, he holds that justice is a virtue and so a good, and that if goodness and justice were separate, injustice would not be an evil. He ends with Scripture: Paul calls the law holy, just and good, the Old Testament calls God good, and Jesus addresses the Father as just.","ideas":["The punishments of the flood, Sodom and the wilderness were remedial, with hope held out afterwards","God confers benefits justly and punishes with kindness","Goodness is the genus of which justice and holiness are species","Both Testaments call the one God both good and just"],"terms":[{"t":"genus and species","d":"Origen reads Paul as naming goodness as the general virtue and justice and holiness as its particular kinds."}]},{"i":123,"j":131,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 6","title":"How the Word of God became man","summary":"Origen turns to the incarnation, marvelling that the Word and Wisdom through whom all things were made lived within a man in Judea, wept as an infant, and died. He explains that one rational soul clung to the Word with perfect love from the beginning and became one spirit with it; this soul is the intermediary between God and flesh, since God cannot mingle with a body directly. Its union was the reward of its love of righteousness, and long, unbroken love turned what had been choice into nature, so that it could not sin. He illustrates this with iron that stays in the fire until it becomes wholly fire, and with Christ's soul as the vessel holding the ointment whose scent the prophets and apostles share. He closes with the shadow of Christ in Lamentations and offers his views as provisional.","ideas":["Christ had a true rational soul, of the same nature as other souls","The soul of Christ mediates between the divine nature and the flesh","Iron in the fire: the soul is so united to the Word that it cannot change","Divine names are given to the human nature and human names to the divine"],"terms":[{"t":"Mediator","d":"The being between God and all created things, which Paul calls the first-born of every creature."},{"t":"oil of gladness","d":"The anointing of Psalm 45, which Origen reads as the soul of Christ being filled with the Holy Spirit and the full Word."}]},{"i":132,"j":137,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 7","title":"The one Holy Spirit, the Paraclete","summary":"Origen returns briefly to the Holy Spirit, insisting that the same Spirit was in the prophets and in the apostles; even the heretics who teach two Gods have not taught two Spirits. All rational creatures share in the Spirit, but its chief outpouring came after Christ's ascension, fulfilling Joel; since then many ordinary believers grasp that the law is to be read spiritually, not literally. The Spirit becomes for each person the gift that person needs, such as wisdom, knowledge or faith. Some who misread the name Paraclete have stirred up division and taught abstinence from marriage and meats. Origen explains that the Paraclete is called comforter when said of the Spirit and intercessor when said of the Saviour.","ideas":["One and the same Spirit in both Testaments","After Christ the Spirit is poured on all flesh, not only on prophets","The Spirit gives each person the gift suited to them","The Spirit's teaching brings consolation by revealing the reasons of things"],"terms":[{"t":"Paraclete","d":"Greek title meaning both comforter and intercessor; comforter of the Spirit, intercessor of Christ."}]},{"i":138,"j":144,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 8","title":"On the soul and its cooling","summary":"Origen defines soul as a substance capable of imagination and impulse and finds souls in all animals, in human beings, in Christ, and perhaps in angels; Scripture even speaks of a soul of God. Noting that Paul links prayer with spirit and understanding rather than soul, and that Scripture seldom praises the soul, he suggests that the Greek psychē may come from growing cold. On this view the understanding fell from its divine warmth and became soul, and if restored it becomes understanding again; minds fell to different degrees, which explains differences in natural ability. The soul of God may mean the only-begotten Son. He repeatedly says these are matters for inquiry, not dogma.","ideas":["Soul as a substance that perceives and moves itself","God is fire, the saints are fervent, the devil and fallen souls are cold","Understanding falls into soul and may return to understanding","Christ's soul stands between weak flesh and willing spirit"],"terms":[{"t":"psychē (soul)","d":"Origen tentatively links the word with cooling, the soul being a mind cooled from divine fervour."},{"t":"nous (understanding)","d":"The rational mind in its original condition, before its fall into soul."}]},{"i":145,"j":149,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 9, §§1–4","title":"Created minds and the diversity of the world","summary":"Origen returns to the beginning of creation: God made a definite number of rational minds, since an infinite number could not be grasped or governed, and created matter in fitting measure. Because these minds came into being from nothing, their goodness was a gift, and with free will some grew negligent and fell away from good to various degrees. He then surveys the variety of the world: heavenly beings of different glory, human beings born free or enslaved, healthy or sick, among civilised or savage peoples, and unequal invisible powers. Since all was made in Christ, who is Righteousness, this diversity must be just, and he asks the Word for light to show in general terms how.","ideas":["Rational creatures are finite in number so that God can govern them","Created goodness is a gift that can be lost through sloth","The world is full of inequality among heavenly, human and invisible beings","Creation in Christ means nothing in it is unjust or accidental"],"terms":[{"t":"number and measure","d":"Number applies to rational minds and measure to bodily matter, both set by God."}]},{"i":150,"j":153,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 9, §§5–8","title":"Diversity of birth arises from merit","summary":"Followers of Marcion, Valentinus and Basilides object that a just God would not create beings of unequal rank or have people born among Hebrews, Greeks or cannibal nations, so they either posit souls of different natures or chance. Origen replies that God, being good and unchanging, created all minds equal and alike; diversity arises from each mind's free movement toward imitation of God or toward negligence. God then arranged them by merit in one world, like a house with vessels of gold and of clay. Paul's case of Jacob and Esau, loved and hated before birth, shows this is not unjust if merit came before bodily birth. As the judgment will sort the good from the bad, so a similar sorting once took place.","ideas":["All rational creatures were first made equal","Diversity comes from free will, not from the Creator or from different natures","Jacob and Esau show causes of merit that precede birth","Some higher beings serve in the world for the sake of lower ones"],"terms":[{"t":"vessels of honour and dishonour","d":"Paul's image of the great house, used for souls placed in the world according to their purity."}]},{"i":154,"j":157,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 10, §§1–3","title":"The resurrection of a spiritual body","summary":"Before discussing punishment Origen considers what body rises. Against heretics who deny bodily resurrection, he argues that what died was a body, that Paul speaks of a spiritual body rising, and that we must be clothed again with our own bodies, now freed from corruption. He asks them to describe the shape of a spiritual body and reads Paul's heavenly and earthly comparisons as the differing glories of saints and of sinners. Against simple believers who expect flesh and blood with its passions, he says flesh and blood will not inherit the kingdom. Like a dead grain, the body keeps a germ from which God restores a body fitting each person's merit; even the bodies of the punished become incorruptible.","ideas":["The body that falls is the body that rises, changed","Heavenly bodies stand for the saints, earthly flesh for sinners","The seed image: a lasting germ in the body is restored by God's word","The risen body cannot be destroyed even by punishment"],"terms":[{"t":"spiritual body","d":"The body raised in glory and incorruption from the animal body that was sown."}]},{"i":158,"j":162,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 10, §§4–8","title":"Hell fire, outer darkness and remedial punishment","summary":"Origen reads Isaiah's 'light of your own fire' to mean that each sinner kindles his own flame, fed by his sins as wood, hay and stubble. Conscience, shown the history of its deeds, accuses itself; the soul also suffers when torn out of the order and harmony in which it was made, like limbs wrenched apart. God acts as a physician who uses bitter drugs, the knife and even fire, and the prophets describe God's wrath and burning as cleansing. The steward 'cut asunder' may mean the withdrawal of the Spirit's gift, the separation of the soul's better part, or the loss of a guardian angel. Outer darkness is profound ignorance, and perhaps dark bodies for the wicked.","ideas":["Sin itself supplies the fuel of punishment","Torment of conscience and of a soul out of harmony","Divine punishment is medicine aimed at cure","Outer darkness as ignorance rather than a dark place"],"terms":[{"t":"purgation","d":"Cleansing of the soul, which Origen sees as the purpose of God's fire and wrath."}]},{"i":163,"j":166,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 11, §§1–3","title":"Carnal and spiritual hopes of the promises","summary":"Origen begins from the claim that every living thing, and above all a rational one, is always in motion, whether toward bodily pleasure, the public good, or the search for truth. Some believers, reading only the letter, expect after the resurrection eating, drinking, marriage, a rebuilt jeweled Jerusalem, foreign servants and earthly kingship; he calls this a Jewish sense of Scripture unworthy of the promises. Those who read as the apostles did expect the bread of life and the wine of Wisdom. The mind, fed on this, is restored to the image and likeness of God, and the princes of that city are those who teach others divine things.","ideas":["Three kinds of human pursuit: pleasure, public service, wisdom","A literal reading looks for earthly pleasures and power after the resurrection","The spiritual reading looks for the food of truth and wisdom","The heavenly Jerusalem as a place where souls are educated"],"terms":[{"t":"bread of life","d":"The food of truth and wisdom on which the saints will feed."}]},{"i":167,"j":170,"loc":"Book II, Chapter 11, §§4–7","title":"The soul's ascent through schools of knowledge","summary":"Origen holds that God implanted in the mind a natural desire to know the causes of his works, as the eye seeks light, and that this desire must at last be satisfied; study in this life is like the outline sketched before a painting is coloured. Paul's longing to depart and be with Christ points to the knowledge he would gain: the meaning of Israel, the priesthood, festivals and sacrifices, the nature of animals and herbs, demons, and providence down to the sparrows. The saints first go to paradise, a school of souls, then rise through the air and the heavenly spheres, learning what is done in each and why. At the end the perfected mind feeds on the contemplation of God in due measure.","ideas":["The desire for truth is from God and will be fulfilled","Earthly learning is a preliminary sketch of perfect knowledge","Paradise as a school or lecture room for departed souls","Ascent through the many mansions to contemplation of God"],"terms":[{"t":"spheres","d":"The Greek name for the celestial regions that Scripture calls heavens, through which the saints pass."}]},{"i":171,"j":173,"loc":"Book III, Preface of Rufinus","title":"Rufinus introduces the last two books","summary":"Rufinus tells Macarius that he translated the first two books quickly during Lent at his urging and the last two more slowly. He notes that, as he foresaw, some were angry that he did not speak ill of Origen, and he expects more uproar since these books expose the hidden ways of demons. He repeats his principle: passages contrary to Origen's other views and to the faith he has left out as interpolations by others, while discussions of rational creatures he has kept, trimming only repetitions. Difficult passages, he says, should be explained by skilled teachers rather than rashly condemned.","ideas":["Translation made at the request of Macarius","Expected hostility to the translation","Omission of passages Rufinus judged interpolated","Hard matters need a qualified interpreter"],"terms":[]},{"i":174,"j":180,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§1–5","title":"Reason and the power of choice","summary":"Because the Church teaches a just judgment, which assumes we can choose good or evil, Origen turns to free will. He classifies moving things: lifeless things are moved from outside, living things from within, and animals by impressions that rouse impulse, as the spider is moved to weave. Rational beings also have reason, which judges these impressions and approves or rejects them, making praise and blame fitting. External things happen to us without our control, but how we use them is ours; a man resolved on chastity is not forced by a tempting woman. Blaming outside causes or bodily make-up makes us like sticks and stones, and conversion and corruption of character show that reason decides.","ideas":["Types of motion: from without, from within, by impression","Reason judges impressions and directs impulses","External stimuli are not in our power, their use is","Changes of character disprove bodily determinism"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (impression)","d":"A presentation that arises in an animal and stirs it to movement."}]},{"i":181,"j":183,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§6–8","title":"Scripture on free will and hard texts","summary":"Origen gathers Scripture for free will: Micah, Moses' two ways, Isaiah, the Psalms, Christ's commands and the parable of the two houses, the last judgment, and Paul in Romans 2. He then lists the passages that seem to deny it: the hardening of Pharaoh, Ezekiel's heart of stone, the parables spoken 'lest they be converted', 'not of him that wills', 'to will and to do are of God', and the potter. Beginning with Pharaoh, he answers heretics who say souls are by nature saved or lost: if Pharaoh were of an earthly nature he would not need hardening many times. Likewise those saved by nature would not need mercy.","ideas":["Commands and judgment presuppose the power to obey","Five sets of texts that appear to remove choice","Repeated hardening implies Pharaoh could have yielded","Mercy implies those saved could have perished"],"terms":[{"t":"earthly and spiritual natures","d":"The heretics' view that some souls are made for destruction and others for salvation."}]},{"i":184,"j":186,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§9–11","title":"How a good God hardens Pharaoh","summary":"Origen asks those who call the God of the law only just how he could justly harden a man and then condemn him. He answers with Paul's image of rain from Hebrews: one rain falls on all ground, producing fruit on tilled land and thorns on neglected land, so God's signs soften some wills and harden others. Likewise the sun melts wax and hardens mud by one heat; the miracles revealed Pharaoh's hardness and the obedience of the Egyptians who left with Israel. Pharaoh's gradual yielding shows the signs did affect him. The phrase is figurative, as when a kind master says he spoiled a slave by his patience.","ideas":["One act of God, different results according to the recipient","Rain on cultivated and neglected soil","The sun melts wax and hardens mud","Hardening as God's forbearance being abused"],"terms":[{"t":"tropical (figurative) sense","d":"The non-literal meaning of a phrase, which must be grasped before faulting the words."}]},{"i":187,"j":189,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§12–14","title":"Delayed cure and the rocky ground","summary":"The prophets' cry 'why have you hardened our heart' means: why did you spare us and let our sin grow? God chastens those he receives as sons and leaves unchastened those not yet ready, until they know their own sickness, since a person who credits himself with God's gifts falls into pride, as the devil did. Because the soul is immortal, God may delay its healing, as physicians delay closing a wound so poison can drain. The rocky ground of the parable is a soul hardened by its own negligence; a wise farmer sows it slowly. God alone knows each soul's cure, and his care for Pharaoh did not end when he drowned.","ideas":["Being left unpunished can be a form of abandonment","Knowledge of one's weakness is needed for healing","God deals with immortal souls over endless ages","No heart is made stony by God; it becomes so by wickedness"],"terms":[]},{"i":190,"j":192,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§15–17","title":"The heart of stone and veiled parables","summary":"Opponents read Ezekiel's promise to replace stony hearts with hearts of flesh as proof that only God removes wickedness. Origen replies with the pupil who submits to a teacher: the teacher promises to remove ignorance only from one willing to obey, as Christ healed those who came asking. On Christ speaking in parables 'lest they be converted', he first notes that the heretics excuse such words in the Gospel while condemning similar ones in the Old Testament. He then argues that a too rapid cure may lead to relapse and worse guilt, so Christ veiled his teaching from those outside. The Tyrians, worse still, heard nothing, their cure postponed for their own good.","ideas":["God's promise of a new heart requires our approach","The same standard must be applied to both Testaments","Withholding clear teaching can protect from a worse relapse","Those who scorn great gifts receive heavier judgment"],"terms":[{"t":"those without","d":"Hearers addressed in parables, close to but distinct from the disciples within."}]},{"i":193,"j":195,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§18–19","title":"Willing, running and God's mercy","summary":"On 'not of him that wills, nor of him that runs', Origen first asks whether desiring good is good; it cannot be blameworthy or indifferent, so a supposedly evil nature could not produce it. He then explains the text by examples: unless the Lord builds the house the builders labour in vain, yet builders still work; a besieged city is saved by God though the guards keep watch; Paul plants, Apollos waters, God gives the growth; sailors credit God with saving the ship. So our effort is needed but the completion is ascribed to God. On 'to will and to do are of God', he says God gives the general power of willing and acting, which we use well or badly.","ideas":["Desiring the good is itself virtuous","Human effort is real but God's part is far greater","Building, siege, farming and sailing as illustrations","God gives the faculty of will, not its particular misuse"],"terms":[]},{"i":196,"j":199,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Rufinus), §§20–22","title":"The potter and vessels of honour","summary":"Some read Paul's potter as making men for salvation or ruin. Origen replies that Paul blames the sinner at Corinth, praises Onesiphorus, and says all will be judged by deeds, and elsewhere says whoever purges himself becomes a vessel of honour. So vessels are made to honour or dishonour from prior causes, which also explains Jacob and Esau. The one lump is the single nature of rational souls; the rebuke 'who are you that replies against God' is for the faultfinder, not the faithful. Against the doctrine of different natures, he holds that souls may rise or fall across ages and worlds. He concludes that our acts need God's help and God's work requires our will.","ideas":["Prior merit explains being formed to honour or dishonour","All rational souls share one nature","Vessels can change rank in a future world","Human will and divine help work together"],"terms":[]},{"i":200,"j":206,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Greek), §§1–5","title":"Free will defined: the Greek text","summary":"The Greek version of the same chapter opens by tying free will to the Church's teaching on just judgment. It sets out the kinds of motion: things moved from outside, things with an inner cause, and animals moved by impressions, such as the spider's urge to weave and the bee's to make wax. Rational beings add reason, which judges impressions and makes praise and blame fitting. Meeting an external stimulus is not up to us, but how we use it is; the trained man resists a tempting woman where another yields. Blaming outside causes or bodily constitution destroys free will, and reformation of character shows reason's power.","ideas":["Animals act by impression, rational beings by judging impressions","Praise and blame follow the use of reason","External events are not ours; our response is","Disciplined reason can transform character"],"terms":[{"t":"hēgemonikon (controlling principle)","d":"The ruling part of the soul whose assent turns an impression into action."}]},{"i":207,"j":209,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Greek), §§6–8","title":"Scriptural witness and the problem texts","summary":"The Greek text cites Micah, Moses, Isaiah, the Psalms, Christ's commands, the houses on rock and sand, the judgment, and Romans 2 to show that living well is our own doing. It then lists the texts that seem to annul free will: Pharaoh hardened, the heart of stone, the parables, 'not of him that wills', 'to will and to do', 'the persuasion is of him that calls', and the potter. Turning to Pharaoh, it answers those who teach natures that cannot be saved or lost: an earthy nature would need no repeated hardening. And those saved by nature would need no second mercy.","ideas":["God's commands assume the power to keep them","The difficult texts set out one by one","The case against fixed natures drawn from Pharaoh","Mercy implies the possibility of perishing"],"terms":[]},{"i":210,"j":214,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Greek), §§9–13","title":"Rain, sun and the long-suffering God","summary":"Origen challenges those who call God only just to explain how hardening can be just. Using the image from Hebrews, one rain yields crops on tilled ground and thorns on neglected ground; one heat melts wax and dries mud; so Moses' signs exposed Pharaoh's hardness and won over the mixed multitude. Pharaoh's partial yielding shows the signs had an effect, and the phrase is like a master saying he spoiled his slaves. The prophets' 'why have you hardened us' means God spared them too long. God delays healing, as a physician leaves an inflammation to draw out hidden poison, because he governs immortal souls over an endless age.","ideas":["Hardening results from wickedness meeting God's goodness","God leaves sinners unpunished so they come to know themselves","Pride in God's gifts caused the devil's fall","Slow cure is surer than quick relapse"],"terms":[]},{"i":215,"j":218,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Greek), §§14–17","title":"Rocky soil, new hearts and parables","summary":"The rocky ground is a soul hardened by negligence; the great Husbandman sows it slowly so the crop is not shallow, and his care for Pharaoh continues even after the drowning. Ezekiel's new heart is promised to those who come of their own accord, as a pupil submits to a teacher or the sick ask Christ for healing. On the parables spoken 'lest they be converted', Origen first shows that the heretics treat similar texts in the two Testaments unequally, which proves both are from one God. He then argues that Christ withheld clearer teaching from those who would relapse after a quick conversion, and that the Tyrians were reserved for a later, more lasting repentance.","ideas":["God alone knows the time and means for each soul","Coming to the physician is our part, healing is Christ's","Parables protect unready hearers from worse guilt","Those who scorned miracles are left without excuse"],"terms":[]},{"i":219,"j":220,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Greek), §§18–19","title":"Human effort and divine completion","summary":"Origen argues that desiring and running after good cannot be blameworthy or indifferent, so a perishing nature could not do it. The Psalm on the Lord building the house and guarding the city does not forbid building or watching but shows that without God they are vain. Paul's planting and watering, and the sailors who credit Providence for bringing the ship to harbour, show that our part is real but God's is infinitely greater. Otherwise commands, praise and blame would be pointless. 'To will and to do are of God' means we receive the general power of will and motion, which we turn to good or bad ends.","ideas":["Desiring the good is a virtuous act","God's share in salvation far exceeds ours","Navigation as an image of providence and skill","The generic power to will is from God, its direction from us"],"terms":[]},{"i":221,"j":224,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 1 (Greek), §§20–22","title":"The potter and the one lump","summary":"If the potter makes some men for salvation and others for ruin, Paul would contradict himself in blaming the Corinthian sinner, praising Onesiphorus and teaching judgment by deeds. Since whoever purges himself becomes a vessel of honour, vessels are formed from older causes, which can also explain Jacob loved and Esau hated before birth. The one lump is the single nature of rational beings, and the rebuke 'who are you' falls on the faultfinder, not on one like Moses. Vessels may change rank in another world, as Israelites may fall and Egyptians and Idumeans rise. Origen concludes that neither our power without God's knowledge nor God's will without our choice makes a vessel of honour.","ideas":["Paul does not contradict himself on blame and praise","Older causes before this life explain inequality","One nature of all rational souls","Salvation joins our will and God's work"],"terms":[]},{"i":225,"j":229,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 2 (§§1–3)","title":"The opposing powers and the origin of sin","summary":"Origen gathers Old and New Testament passages showing that the devil and hostile powers contend with humanity, from the serpent and Eve to Satan entering Judas. He rejects the view of simpler believers that every sin is caused by demons: hunger, thirst and sexual desire arise from the body, and excess in them can begin without the devil. The hostile powers seize on such first lapses and push them further, as small desire for money grows into avarice and violence. Citing Paul, he argues that God, like a fair president of the games, matches each person with temptations proportioned to his strength, giving the power to resist but leaving its use to free will.","ideas":["Scripture testifies throughout to spirits opposing the human race","Some sins arise from natural bodily movements, not demons","Demons exploit and enlarge an initial intemperance","Temptation is proportioned to strength; victory depends on free will"],"terms":[{"t":"Apopompæus (Averter)","d":"The name in Leviticus for the figure who receives the second lot, which Origen lists among hostile powers."},{"t":"free will","d":"The power, given by God, to use one's strength diligently or slothfully, which makes victory praiseworthy and defeat blameworthy."}]},{"i":230,"j":233,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 2 (§§4–7)","title":"Where thoughts come from; the spiritual contest","summary":"Thoughts and recollections, Origen argues, arise sometimes from ourselves, sometimes from hostile powers, and sometimes from God or angels; he cites the Psalms, the Shepherd's two angels and Barnabas's two ways. Such suggestions only stir the mind, and the will remains free to reject evil or refuse good. No one fights all the powers at once; each soldier of Christ meets particular adversaries, and no one conquers without divine help, as Jacob's helping angel shows. The contest is spirit against spirit: losses and calumnies, as in Job's case, aim to drive us to despair or blasphemy, and such events happen not by God, yet not without His permission.","ideas":["Thoughts may be suggested by self, demons, angels or God","Suggestions only incite; the will stays free","Each person fights limited adversaries and needs divine aid","Misfortunes are permitted by God, not caused by Him"],"terms":[{"t":"fiery darts","d":"Paul's image for the devil's assaults on a heart left open by sloth, quenched only by the shield of faith."}]},{"i":234,"j":241,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 3","title":"Threefold wisdom and the working of spirits","summary":"From 1 Corinthians Origen distinguishes the wisdom of this world, the wisdom of the princes of this world, and the wisdom of God revealed more fully in Christ. Worldly wisdom covers arts like poetry, grammar, rhetoric, geometry, music and perhaps medicine; the princes' wisdom is Egyptian occult philosophy, Chaldean and Indian astrology and Greek opinions about the divine, taught by spiritual powers set over nations. He suspects these princes teach what they believe true, while other apostate powers devise heresies from envy. Spirits may possess a soul wholly or corrupt it by suggestion, while good spirits inspire without disturbing the mind; the differing influences on souls from birth he traces to merits earlier than bodily life.","ideas":["Three kinds of wisdom: of the world, of its princes, of God","Spiritual powers inspire arts, divination and false doctrine","Good inspiration leaves the mind calm and the will free","Prenatal differences point to prior causes in souls"],"terms":[{"t":"princes of this world","d":"Spiritual powers, such as the princes of Persia and Tyre, each ruling a nation and spreading its own wisdom."},{"t":"energy (operation)","d":"The working of a good or evil spirit received by a soul while in the body."}]},{"i":242,"j":249,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 4","title":"Human temptations and the question of two souls","summary":"Origen asks whether temptation from flesh and blood implies a second element in us inclined to evil. He sets out three views: two souls, one heavenly and one earthly; the pull of a lifeless body; and the Greek tripartite soul, which he finds little supported by Scripture. Advocates of two souls argue from Paul's flesh lusting against the Spirit and the works of the flesh, making the soul's own will a lukewarm middle that is better joined to spirit, or even to flesh, than left to itself. The opposing side holds there is one soul, explaining these phrases as figures of speech for bodily needs, and asks how a good Creator could make a nature hostile to Himself. Origen leaves the choice to the reader.","ideas":["Three accounts of inner conflict are laid out","The 'tepid' soul between flesh and spirit","One-soul view reads 'wisdom of the flesh' as catachresis","Origen presents both sides and lets the reader choose"],"terms":[{"t":"traducianism","d":"The view that the lower soul is generated from bodily seed along with the body."},{"t":"catachresis","d":"An improper use of words, as when earth is said to 'wish' for water."}]},{"i":250,"j":259,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 5","title":"The world's beginning in time","summary":"Origen affirms the Church's teaching that the world began in time and will end, citing Genesis, Jacob, the Psalms, Christ and Paul, and arguing that only what has a beginning can be comprehended by God. To the question of what God did before, he answers that other worlds preceded this one and others will follow, citing Isaiah and Ecclesiastes. The term katabolē signals a descent of rational beings from invisible to visible conditions, some by their faults and some, like the stars, unwillingly sent to serve. In the last times the Son took the form of a servant to restore obedience and rule; His final subjection to the Father, and the subjection of enemies to Him, mean their salvation, achieved by persuasion and training, not force.","ideas":["The world had a beginning and will end","Successive worlds before and after this one","Katabolē as a casting down from higher to lower","Subjection to Christ means restoration, not compulsion"],"terms":[{"t":"katabolē","d":"Greek for the world's 'foundation', which Origen reads as a casting downwards."},{"t":"subjection","d":"The Son's and all creatures' final submission to the Father, read as perfected happiness."}]},{"i":260,"j":269,"loc":"Book III, Chapter 6","title":"The end of the world and the spiritual body","summary":"The highest good, Origen says, is likeness to God, a goal he finds in Moses before the philosophers: man received the image at creation and must attain the likeness by his own effort. 'God all in all' means that every purified mind will think and feel only God, with evil gone and the end restored to the beginning. Death, the last enemy, will be destroyed not in substance but as an enemy; the body is not annihilated but transformed, gradually over countless ages, into a spiritual body, the same body raised in glory, against the Greek fifth body. A heavenly land will train the saints toward perfection before God becomes all in all; Origen leaves the details to the reader.","ideas":["Image given at creation, likeness achieved at the end","God all in all: every purified mind wholly filled with God","Death destroyed as enemy, not as substance","Same body transformed into a spiritual body by degrees"],"terms":[{"t":"image and likeness","d":"Origen's distinction between the dignity given at creation and the perfection reached at the end."},{"t":"spiritual body","d":"The present body transformed into a glorious, incorruptible condition."}]},{"i":270,"j":279,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§1–7 (Rufinus)","title":"Proofs that the Scriptures are divine","summary":"Beginning Book IV in Rufinus's Latin, Origen argues that Scripture is inspired. No Greek or barbarian lawgiver won foreign nations to his laws, yet countless people across the world have embraced Moses and Christ despite persecution, with few unskilled teachers, as Christ foretold. Prophecies are fulfilled: rulers have failed from Judah since the temple's fall, a foolish nation of Gentiles has been chosen, and the Psalms, Isaiah, Micah and Daniel point to Christ. Christ's coming revealed the inspiration of the law and prophets; that its divinity is not obvious everywhere is no more an objection than the hiddenness of providence, since the treasure lies in earthen vessels.","ideas":["The worldwide spread of the faith exceeds human power","Fulfilled prophecy confirms Christ and Scripture","Christ's advent removed the veil from the law","Hidden meaning, like hidden providence, does not disprove inspiration"],"terms":[{"t":"Patriarch","d":"The Jewish leader whom some claim continues Judah's rule, which Origen denies."}]},{"i":280,"j":286,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§8–13 (Rufinus)","title":"Literal misreading and the threefold sense","summary":"Misreading Scripture literally, Origen says, led Jews to reject Christ because animal prophecies were not fulfilled, led heretics to posit a merely just Creator below a higher good God, and leads simple believers to think unworthy things of God. All admit Scripture holds mysteries, such as Lot's daughters or the tabernacle, though their sense is hard to find; the Gospels, Revelation and Epistles too need the key of knowledge. Following Proverbs' 'threefold' rule, he assigns Scripture a body for the simple, a soul for the advancing and a spirit for the perfect, illustrated by Hermas's two books and the water-jars of Cana. Paul's readings of the muzzled ox, the Rock, Hagar and Sarah, and the law's shadows model the higher senses.","ideas":["Literalism produced Jewish unbelief and heresy","Scripture has body, soul and spirit like a human being","Some passages lack any bodily sense","Paul's allegories show the spiritual method"],"terms":[{"t":"body, soul, spirit of Scripture","d":"The historical sense, the moral sense, and the spiritual sense for the perfect."}]},{"i":287,"j":292,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§14–18 (Rufinus)","title":"Stumbling-blocks placed in the letter","summary":"The Spirit's first aim, Origen holds, was to teach about God, the Son, rational creatures, souls and evil; its second was to veil these in histories and laws that still profit the many. Lest readers stop at the surface, divine wisdom inserted impossibilities and incongruities into narrative and law as a bolt barring the literal path. He cites days without a sun, a tree eaten for life, God walking in paradise, the devil showing all kingdoms from one mountain, the goat-stag and griffin, the Sabbath rule never to move, and Gospel precepts such as turning the left cheek. Such things show that the letter alone cannot always edify.","ideas":["Scripture veils mysteries under history and law","Deliberate impossibilities force the search for deeper sense","Examples drawn from Genesis, the law, Gospels and Paul"],"terms":[{"t":"stumbling-blocks","d":"Impossibilities inserted by the Spirit to turn readers from the surface to the inner meaning."}]},{"i":293,"j":298,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§19–23 (Rufinus)","title":"Literal truth retained; heavenly Israel and Jerusalem","summary":"Origen insists most history is literally true, like the burial at Hebron, and many commands, like honouring parents and not swearing, must be kept as written, though careful readers will sometimes hesitate. He then applies the method: Paul's 'Israel after the flesh' implies a spiritual Israel, a race of souls 'seeing God', whose metropolis is the heavenly Jerusalem. Prophecies about Egypt, Babylon, Tyre and Nebuchadnezzar as Lucifer fit spiritual places better than earthly ones. Souls may descend from those places into this world according to merit, so that an Israelite may be born among Scythians; the literal sense is the field, the spiritual the hidden treasure.","ideas":["Historical passages outnumber purely spiritual ones","Israel and Jerusalem signify souls and a heavenly city","Souls descend into births according to desert","Letter as field, spiritual sense as buried treasure"],"terms":[{"t":"Israel","d":"Interpreted as 'a mind, or man seeing God', naming a race of souls."},{"t":"Ebionites","d":"Literalist sect who took Christ's mission to Israel carnally; their name means 'poor'."}]},{"i":299,"j":307,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§1–7 (from the Greek)","title":"Inspiration of Scripture: the Greek text","summary":"A deliberately literal translation of the surviving Greek is given so it may be compared with Rufinus. It argues, as before, that Moses and Jesus alone won zealous followers among many nations despite hatred and death, and that the word spread in a few years beyond human power. Jacob's prophecy about Judah, now without king or sacrifice, against claims for the Ethnarch, the foolish nation of Deuteronomy, the Psalm for the Beloved, Isaiah's Emmanuel, Micah, Daniel and Job are read as fulfilled in Jesus. The prophets' divinity shone forth after Christ, and Scripture's hiddenness parallels that of providence: faith rests on the Spirit's power, not persuasive words.","ideas":["Parallel Greek version of the opening argument","Success of the gospel shows divine origin","Prophecies fulfilled in Jesus prove Scripture inspired","Treasure in earthen vessels: plain words, divine power"],"terms":[{"t":"Ethnarch","d":"The Jewish ruler whom opponents cited as Judah's continuing line."}]},{"i":308,"j":313,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§8–13 (from the Greek)","title":"Errors of the letter and the threefold method","summary":"The Greek text names the heretics' lower god the Demiurge, whom they think imperfect, and says literalism caused both Jewish rejection of Jesus and unworthy ideas of God among simple believers. Those who see types in the patriarchs' marriages and the tabernacle are right that they are types, but often err in applying them. Prophecies, Gospels, Revelation and Epistles all need the key of knowledge. Scripture is to be portrayed threefold in the soul: flesh for the simple, soul for the advancing, spirit for the perfect, illustrated by Hermas, the Cana water-jars and Paul's allegories of the ox, the Rock, Hagar and Sarah, and the shadows of the law.","ideas":["Literalism lies behind Jewish, heretical and naive errors","Scripture, like man, has flesh, soul and spirit","Paul's allegories as models of the spiritual sense"],"terms":[{"t":"Demiurge","d":"The Creator God, whom the heretics wrongly separate from and set beneath a higher God."}]},{"i":314,"j":319,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§14–18 (from the Greek)","title":"The Spirit's two aims and literal impossibilities","summary":"The Greek states the Spirit's chief aim as teaching ineffable mysteries about God, His Son, rational beings divine and fallen, souls, the world and evil; its second aim was to clothe these in profitable histories and laws. Because a seamless surface would keep readers on the letter, the Word wove in events that did not happen, or could not, and laws irrational or impossible. Examples include days before the sun, the trees of paradise, the mountain of temptation, the ban on eating vultures, the goat-stag, the Sabbath rules with their rabbinic and Samaritan interpretations, the struck right cheek and the offending right eye. The design is that the letter should not be received alone.","ideas":["Mysteries clothed in edifying narratives","Non-events and impossible laws are woven in deliberately","Gospel and Pauline examples alongside Mosaic ones"],"terms":[{"t":"Dositheus the Samaritan","d":"Cited for the rule that one must stay all Sabbath in the posture one is found in."}]},{"i":320,"j":324,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§19–23 (from the Greek)","title":"Literal truth and the spiritual Israel (Greek)","summary":"Most history and many commands are literally true and binding, such as Hebron, Shechem, the Decalogue and Christ's command not to swear, though the careful reader must search the Scriptures to judge doubtful cases. All of Scripture has a spiritual sense, but not all of it a bodily sense. The Greek adds the inward Jew and the circumcision of the heart, and a spiritual genealogy rising through the patriarchs to Adam, read as Christ, with Eve as the Church. Prophecies of Egypt, Babylon, Tyre and Nebuchadnezzar point to spiritual counterparts; souls 'dying' above descend into this Hades among different peoples, and the hidden treasure in the field is the deeper sense.","ideas":["Whole of Scripture spiritual, not all of it bodily","Inward Jews and a spiritual line of descent","Souls descend into earthly births by merit"],"terms":[{"t":"Hades","d":"Here this earthly region, into which souls 'dying' above descend."}]},{"i":325,"j":329,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter 1 §§24–27 (from the Latin)","title":"Mysteries of Exodus; the limits of knowledge","summary":"Returning to the Latin, Origen reads the fathers' descent into Egypt as providence enlightening others, and the plagues, wilderness, tabernacle and priestly robes as shadows of heavenly things. The numbering of fighting men signifies soldiers of Christ; those who fell in the desert resemble souls that progressed but did not reach perfection; Deuteronomy, the second law given to Joshua, figures the Gospel and perhaps Christ's glorious second coming. Like Paul crying out at the depth of God's wisdom, no created mind can comprehend everything; his Hebrew teacher read Isaiah's two seraphim as the Son and Spirit alone knowing beginning and end. Readers should attend to meaning, not words, as with the incorporeal Trinity.","ideas":["Exodus and Numbers as figures of spiritual warfare","Deuteronomy points to the Gospel and second advent","No created mind reaches the end of inquiry","Meaning matters more than words"],"terms":[{"t":"asōmaton (incorporeal)","d":"The Greek term for what Scripture calls invisible; the Trinity alone is wholly incorporeal."},{"t":"everlasting Gospel","d":"The law under which the saints will live in the kingdom of heaven."}]},{"i":330,"j":340,"loc":"Book IV, Summary §§28–32","title":"Summary: Trinity, incarnation and participation","summary":"Origen recapitulates. The Son is not a 'prolation' from the Father, nor made from nothing, but begotten incorporeally like an act of will, as splendour from light, so there was never a time He was not, a phrase itself beyond time. As Word and Wisdom He is in no place yet in all the saints and angels, more fully in some according to merit. In the incarnation His divinity was neither confined to His body nor divided; He took a human soul which, choosing the good before knowing evil, was united with the Word and anointed above its fellows, an example for believers. Every rational creature becomes holy by participation in the one incorporeal Trinity.","ideas":["Eternal generation of the Son without bodily process","Christ present in all beings according to their merits","Christ's sinless human soul united to the Word","All rational creatures need participation in the Trinity"],"terms":[{"t":"prolation","d":"Emanation like animal generation, which Origen rejects for the Son."},{"t":"oil of gladness","d":"The anointing of Christ's soul for its love of righteousness and stainless union with the Word."}]},{"i":341,"j":345,"loc":"Book IV, Summary §§33–37","title":"Matter, qualities, and the immortal soul","summary":"Origen notes that canonical Scripture never uses 'matter' for the substrate of bodies, though Wisdom of Solomon and readings of Genesis suggest it. Matter is changeable and created, not unbegotten; it never exists without quality and is grasped only in thought, though some hold bodies are nothing but qualities. God made all by number and measure, creating bodily nature to clothe changeable rational natures as long as they need bodies. Since all minds share the same intellectual light as the immortal heavenly powers and the Trinity, human souls are of one nature with them and incorruptible; the image of God is in virtues, not the body, and keeps seeds of renewal.","ideas":["Matter is created and known only by abstraction","Bodily nature exists to serve changeable rational beings","Participation in divine light implies immortality","A 'divine sense' perceives intellectual things"],"terms":[{"t":"hylē (matter)","d":"The substance underlying bodies, which Origen holds created and never without qualities."},{"t":"participation","d":"Sharing in a common light, which shows that all minds are of one nature."}]},{"i":346,"j":428,"loc":"Elucidations; tables of bishops","title":"Editor's elucidations and episcopal succession lists","summary":"The editor adds notes on the text. He remarks on Origen's appeals to 'the teaching of the Church' and asks whether this reflects Alexandrian leadership, independent of Rome. Further notes cite Bull on the Son's subordination, reject the Filioque as an addition to the Creed, describe pre-Nicene local creeds based on the Jerusalem symbol, praise Edwards and Tappan on the will, and list who did not treat the Wisdom of Solomon as Scripture. Tables compiled from Eusebius list the bishops of Jerusalem from James to Hermon (a.d. 300) and of Alexandria from Annianus to Alexander (a.d. 326).","ideas":["Origen assumes a settled 'faith of the Church'","Editor's anti-Roman and anti-Filioque comments","Wisdom of Solomon's disputed authority","Succession lists for Jerusalem and Alexandria"],"terms":[{"t":"Filioque","d":"The Western addition of procession 'and from the Son', which the editor holds has no rightful place in the Creed."}]}]});